# Copy to .env.local, which is gitignored. Both variables below are read at
# BUILD time and baked into the bundle, so changing either is a rebuild — or a
# restart of `npm run dev` — and never a reload.

# Where the built app sends /api and /ws.
#
# Leave it unset — the normal case — and the app uses its own origin, which is
# right whenever the runner serves it. Set it when the UI is deployed somewhere
# the runner is not:
#
#   VITE_API_URL=https://api.ghostclick.example
#
# Cross-origin also needs the runner told which origin may call it, or the
# browser blocks every request before it leaves. In the backend repository:
#
#   GC_WEB_ORIGIN=https://ghostclick.example  npm start
#
# `npm run dev` needs neither: Vite proxies /api and /ws to GC_API
# (default http://localhost:3000), so the browser only ever sees one origin.

# Where the control plane is — Django, which owns logins.
#
# Leave it unset and there is no login screen and no token: the app runs open
# against a runner that is also unauthenticated, which is the laptop case. Set
# it and every call carries a short-lived token, and the runner must be started
# with the PUBLIC half (GC_AUTH_PUBLIC_KEYS) of the key the control plane signs
# with (GC_SIGNING_KEY). Both live in the backend repository now, where the key
# is minted with `cd auth && python manage.py signing_key --new`.
#
#   VITE_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:8000
#
# Setting this in development also obliges BOTH of those services to name the
# dev server's origin — Django for CORS and CSRF, the runner because a gated
# socket upgrade is accepted only from the origin the UI is served at, and the
# proxy forwards the browser's real Origin on /ws:
#
#   GC_WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5180
#
# README.md has the three commands in full.
